{"name":"Camel Fur","hex":"#bb6600","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammalian beast of burden, much used in desert areas, of the genus Camelus.","A light brownish color, like that of a camel (also called camel brown)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown color like that of a camel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel"},{"term":"fur","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Friulian."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hairy coat of various mammal species, especially when fine, soft and thick.","The hairy skins of animals used as a material for clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fur"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Fur","blocks":[{"text":"Fur is the soft, thick growth of hair that covers the skin of almost all mammals. It consists of a combination of oily guard hair on top and thick underfur beneath. The guard hair keeps moisture from reaching the skin; the underfur acts as an insulating blanket that keeps the animal warm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg/330px-GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fur","wikidata":"Q197204"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Camelcalf-feeding.jpg/330px-Camelcalf-feeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_(color)","wikidata":"Q17082553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/1280px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACamels_by_country_per_capita.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG/500px-2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG/1280px-2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampelti